# Edith

Edith rebuilds and audits this machine. Its source is `{{ edith.repo }}`, and that repo, not `~/.claude`, is where every change starts.

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `edith doctor` | Checks every claim: tools, standing orders, status line, git identity, PATH, stray rules and skills, projects. Exit 1 on any failure |
| `edith tools [group] [--missing]` | What the manifest lists and what's installed |
| `edith install <group>` | Installs a group's missing tools: winget, `uv tool`, or a digest-checked GitHub release |
| `edith deploy [--dry-run]` | Renders the kit into place behind a snapshot. Exit 1 means some files were edited by hand and left alone |
| `edith diff [text]` | The exact lines deploy would change |
| `edith restore [name]` | Lists snapshots, or puts one back; restoring takes its own snapshot |
| `edith projects [--attention]` | Every project under the roots: dirty, unpushed, no remote, no git |
| `edith ship -m "msg"` | Message rules, secret scan, `just check`, commit, push |
| `edith shelve <path>` | Parks unmanaged Claude config (like `rules/ecc`) on a shelf; `unshelve` brings it back |

## Rules

- Never edit `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, the status line, or a kit skill in place: deploy would see the edit as drift. Change the kit in `{{ edith.repo }}/kit`, run `just check` there, then `edith deploy`.
- After `edith install`, run `edith deploy` so the standing orders name the new tools.
- When deploy reports drift, show `edith diff` and ask before `--force`; the hand edit may be worth moving into the kit.
- Commit and push with `edith ship` unless the user asks otherwise. If it refuses, fix the cause: reword the message, remove the secret, make the checks pass. Never route around it.
- To add a tool, give it an entry in `kit/tools.toml` (a `claim` and `area` only if Claude should use it), run `just check`, then `edith install <group>` and `edith deploy`.
- Shelving is for config edith doesn't manage. It moves things and never deletes them.
